<div id=3D"outline-container-4" class=3D"outline-2">
<h2 id=3D"4">Dependent if expressions without dependent types</h2>
<div class=3D"outline-text-2" id=3D"text-4">
<p>
Archive: <a href=3D"https://discuss.ocaml.org/t/dependent-if-expressions-wi=
thout-dependent-types/18506/1">https://discuss.ocaml.org/t/dependent-if-exp=
ressions-without-dependent-types/18506/1</a>
</p>
</div>
<div id=3D"outline-container-org45ff0ce" class=3D"outline-3">
<h3 id=3D"org45ff0ce">Didier Wenzek announced</h3>
<div class=3D"outline-text-3" id=3D"text-org45ff0ce">
<p>
This <a href=3D"https://haskellforall.com/2026/09/dependent-if-expressions"=
>post on Haskell for all</a>
shows an insighful use of Church encoding to implement dependent if express=
ions without dependent types.
</p>

<p>
The following OCaml code type checks and works:
</p>

<div class=3D"org-src-container">
<pre class=3D"src src-ocaml"><code># <span style=3D"color: #242521; font-we=
ight: bold;">let</span> <span style=3D"color: #a7601f;">example</span> <spa=
n style=3D"color: #007a9f;">bool</span> =3D if_then_else bool 5 <span style=
=3D"color: #ca3400;">"hi!"</span><span style=3D"color: #9f0d0f;">;;</span>

# example t<span style=3D"color: #9f0d0f;">;;</span>
 - : int =3D 5
# example f<span style=3D"color: #9f0d0f;">;;</span>
=2D : string =3D <span style=3D"color: #ca3400;">"hi!"</span>
# example (f <span style=3D"color: #9f0d0f;">&amp;&amp;</span> t)<span styl=
e=3D"color: #9f0d0f;">;;</span>
=2D : string =3D <span style=3D"color: #ca3400;">"hi!"</span>
# example (f <span style=3D"color: #9f0d0f;">||</span> t)<span style=3D"col=
or: #9f0d0f;">;;</span>
=2D : int =3D 5
# example (<span style=3D"color: #9f0d0f;">not</span> t)<span style=3D"colo=
r: #9f0d0f;">;;</span>
=2D : string =3D <span style=3D"color: #ca3400;">"hi!"</span>
</code></pre>
</div>

<p>
This is simply based on Hindley-Milner type inference, with a single trick
that is to be not too restrictive on the type for Church encoded booleans.
</p>

<p>
Where the first idea to Church encode booleans would be to restrict the <co=
de>then</code> and <code>else</code> cases to be the same
(using a record to encode the forall type):
</p>

<div class=3D"org-src-container">
<pre class=3D"src src-ocaml"><code><span style=3D"color: #242521; font-weig=
ht: bold;">type</span> <span style=3D"color: #444fcf; font-weight: bold;">b=
ool</span> =3D { check : 'a. 'a -&gt; 'a -&gt; 'a; }
</code></pre>
</div>

<p>
Dependent if expressions require a liberal definition:
</p>

<div class=3D"org-src-container">
<pre class=3D"src src-ocaml"><code><span style=3D"color: #242521; font-weig=
ht: bold;">type</span> <span style=3D"color: #444fcf; font-weight: bold;">b=
ool</span> =3D { check : 'a 'b 'c. 'a -&gt; 'b -&gt; 'c; }
</code></pre>
</div>

<p>
And this is what is inferred when no type is enforced
(ignoring the fact we get then weakly polymorphic types instead of forall t=
ypes):
</p>

<div class=3D"org-src-container">
<pre class=3D"src src-ocaml"><code><span style=3D"color: #242521; font-weig=
ht: bold;">let</span> <span style=3D"color: #a7601f;">t</span> <span style=
=3D"color: #007a9f;">if_branch</span> <span style=3D"color: #007a9f;">else_=
branch</span> =3D if_branch
<span style=3D"color: #242521; font-weight: bold;">let</span> <span style=
=3D"color: #a7601f;">f</span> <span style=3D"color: #007a9f;">if_branch</sp=
an> <span style=3D"color: #007a9f;">else_branch</span> =3D else_branch

<span style=3D"color: #242521; font-weight: bold;">let</span> <span style=
=3D"color: #a7601f;">if_then_else</span> <span style=3D"color: #007a9f;">bo=
ol</span> <span style=3D"color: #007a9f;">if_branch</span> <span style=3D"c=
olor: #007a9f;">else_branch</span> =3D bool if_branch else_branch

<span style=3D"color: #242521; font-weight: bold;">let</span> (<span style=
=3D"color: #a7601f;">&amp;&amp;</span>) <span style=3D"color: #007a9f;">a</=
span> <span style=3D"color: #007a9f;">b</span> <span style=3D"color: #007a9=
f;">if_branch</span> <span style=3D"color: #007a9f;">else_branch</span> =3D=
 a (b if_branch else_branch) else_branch
<span style=3D"color: #242521; font-weight: bold;">let</span> (<span style=
=3D"color: #a7601f;">||</span>) <span style=3D"color: #007a9f;">a</span> <s=
pan style=3D"color: #007a9f;">b</span> <span style=3D"color: #007a9f;">if_b=
ranch</span> <span style=3D"color: #007a9f;">else_branch</span> =3D a if_br=
anch (b if_branch else_branch)
<span style=3D"color: #242521; font-weight: bold;">let</span> <span style=
=3D"color: #a7601f;">not</span> <span style=3D"color: #007a9f;">a</span> <s=
pan style=3D"color: #007a9f;">if_branch</span> <span style=3D"color: #007a9=
f;">else_branch</span> =3D a else_branch if_branch
</code></pre>
</div>

<p>
I encourage you read the full post, this is a really nice read.
</p>
</div>
