<h2 id=3D"6">bstr, slice and bin (bigstring, encoders and decoders for bina=
ry formats)</h2>
<div class=3D"outline-text-2" id=3D"text-6">
<p>
Archive: <a href=3D"https://discuss.ocaml.org/t/ann-bstr-slice-bin-bigstrin=
g-encoders-decoders-for-binary-formats/18509/1">https://discuss.ocaml.org/t=
/ann-bstr-slice-bin-bigstring-encoders-decoders-for-binary-formats/18509/1<=
/a>
</p>
</div>
<div id=3D"outline-container-orgedb3121" class=3D"outline-3">
<h3 id=3D"orgedb3121">Calascibetta Romain announced</h3>
<div class=3D"outline-text-3" id=3D"text-orgedb3121">
<p>
I am delighted to announce the release of <a href=3D"https://github.com/rob=
ur-coop/bstr"><code>bstr.0.1.0</code></a>, as well as <code>bin.0.1.0</code=
> and <code>slice.0.1.0</code>. These releases are the result of a syntheti=
c work between several libraries, aimed at bringing together everything tha=
t might be useful to us in implementing formats and protocols within <a hre=
f=3D"https://robur.coop">our cooperative</a>. In particular, these librarie=
s offer:
</p>
<ul class=3D"org-ul">
<li>a comprehensive module for manipulating what are known as <span class=
=3D"underline">bigstrings</span> (replacing <a href=3D"https://github.com/i=
nhabitedtype/bigstringaf">bigstringaf</a> - because the name of that librar=
y is too long)</li>
<li>a library providing access to bigstrings and bytes (in short, <a href=
=3D"https://github.com/robur-coop/bstr/blob/b427711671c1654df37550849efe526=
5c866ebcc/lib/slice.mli#L1">an abstraction</a> of <a href=3D"https://github=
.com/mirage/ocaml-cstruct">ocaml-cstruct</a>)</li>
<li>finally, a library for describing binary formats from which one can der=
ive an encoder and a decoder (in the spirit of what <a href=3D"https://gith=
ub.com/mirage/repr">repr</a> can offer)</li>
</ul>

<p>
For those who want to understand the benefits of bigstrings, I=E2=80=99ve p=
reviously shared my thoughts on the subject <a href=3D"https://discuss.ocam=
l.org/t/buffered-io-bytes-vs-bigstring/8978/3?u=3Ddinosaure">here</a>. Alth=
ough we=E2=80=99ve since backtracked on the use of <code>ocaml-cstruct</cod=
e>, particularly for performance reasons (see <a href=3D"https://blog.robur=
.coop/articles/miragevpn-performance.html">this article</a>), bigstrings re=
main useful in certain cases: they should, fundamentally, be used wisely.
</p>

<p>
Particular attention has been paid to performance using <a href=3D"https://=
github.com/mirage/bechamel"><code>bechamel</code></a> (for micro-benchmarki=
ng), and we can draw a few conclusions from this:
</p>
<ul class=3D"org-ul">
<li><p>
<code>bstr</code> has the edge over <code>bigstringaf</code> as it uses tag=
s that did not exist at the time <code>bigstringaf</code> was developed
</p>
